WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:05.870
For Spider-Man fans, Into The Spider-Verse
was a non-stop parade of references all revolving

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:07.320
your favorite web slinger.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:12.580
Here are even more small details and easter
eggs you might have missed in Into The Spider-Verse.

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:16.450
And it should go without saying, but we're
saying it anyway: swinging Spidey spoilers

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:16.940
ahead.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:21.960
Brian Michael Bendis, Sarah Pichelli, and
Steve Ditko aren't the only Spider-Man comic

00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:24.600
book creators to get a shout-out in Into The
Spider-Verse.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:29.160
One of the other contacts in Miles' phone
is D. Slott, referencing comic book writer

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.880
Dan Slott, the writer who came up with the
concept of the Spider-Verse for the comic

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:33.380
books.

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:38.720
Meanwhile, Miles walks past a ramen shop called
"Romita Ramen" partway through the film.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:43.360
This is a double shout-out: John Romita Sr.
was the artist who took over the art duties

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:47.860
on Amazing Spider-Man after original Spidey
artist Steve Ditko left the title.

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:52.370
He's also the co-creator of Mary Jane Watson,
who appears in the film, and the original

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:54.320
version of The Prowler, Hobie Brown.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:59.740
His son, John Romita Jr., likewise had a long,
successful run on Amazing Spider-Man.

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:04.439
Into the Spider-Verse is also loaded with
visual references to classic comic arcs and

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:05.540
character designs.

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:11.040
Kingpin dominates the screen whenever he's
shown, in an obvious reference to Bill Sienkiewicz's

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:12.179
famous portrayal.

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:16.680
And when Gwen Stacy's Spider-Woman appears,
the cover of her comic makes sure to credit

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:21.300
her actual creators: Jason Latour, Robbi Rodriguez,
and Rico Renzi.

00:01:21.569 --> 00:01:26.700
It goes further than just a shout-out, too:
Gwen's flashback sequences are washed over

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:31.840
in kinetic neon pinks and purples, a perfect
replication of colorist Rico Renzi's color

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:33.760
scheme in the Spider-Gwen comic.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:38.280
When the various Spider-people explore Aunt
May's secret Spider-Bunker, you might have

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.780
noticed a quick shot of the Spider-Mobile
on one of the platforms.

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:45.700
In the comics, the Spider-Mobile could drive
along walls and shoot webbing.

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:50.240
It didn't last long as Spidey's vehicle of
choice though: it was quickly thrashed, lost,

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:54.420
and, of course, repurposed by a supervillain
to try and kill Spider-Man.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:02:00.020
One of the most subtle Easter eggs in the
film comes when Aunt May sees Olivia Octavius

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:01.300
and calls her "Liv."

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:05.990
As the villain told Spider-Man earlier in
the film, her enemies call her Doc Ock, but

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:07.520
her friends call her "Liv."

00:02:07.789 --> 00:02:12.780
The fact that Aunt May knows and uses Doc
Ock's nickname hints at a previous relationship

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:13.540
between the two.

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:15.700
But what did that relationship look like?

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.640
Well, if Miles' universe is anything like
the regular Marvel Universe, chances are that

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:25.520
Doc Ock and Aunt May may have had a pretty
close relationship...like almost getting married.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:31.260
It all happened in Amazing Spider-Man #131,
which featured the eight-armed menace nearly

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:35.500
marrying Spidey's aunt in order to claim a
nuclear power plant that May received from

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:36.500
a recent inheritance.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.400
May does seem to have a thing for genius inventors.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:42.800
"It's so hard for me to believe that she's
someone's aunt."

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:45.100
"Well, we come in all shapes and sizes."

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:47.700
"This woman's meatloaf is exceptional."

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:48.420
"Let me just stop you there."

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:53.200
Fans were no doubt surprised to see Peter
Parker bite the bullet early on in the film.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:57.700
Fans might have been even more surprised when
Peter takes off his mask and his hair is blond

00:02:57.700 --> 00:02:59.280
instead of the expected brown.

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:03.980
That's because the filmmakers are paying tribute
to the original duplicate Spider-Man, Ben

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:04.480
Reilly.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:09.140
In the comics, Reilly was a clone of Peter
Parker who dyed his hair and changed his name

00:03:09.140 --> 00:03:11.880
to differentiate himself from the original
Peter Parker.

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:17.239
While Shameik Moore does an excellent job
voicing young Miles Morales in the film, many

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:20.900
fans had their hearts set on Donald Glover
to portray the young web-slinger.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:26.210
The Atlanta creator was pushed into the Spider-spotlight
when he half-jokingly campaigned to play the

00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:28.060
role of Spider-Man in 2010.

00:03:28.409 --> 00:03:33.740
While the role ultimately went to Andrew Garfield,
Glover did voice Miles Morales in the Ultimate

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:38.489
Spider-Man cartoon, and he even appeared in
Spider-Man: Homecoming as Miles' uncle, Aaron

00:03:38.489 --> 00:03:38.980
Davis.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:43.300
"The other night you told those guys if you're
gonna shoot somebody, shoot me.

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:44.340
Pretty ballsy.

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:48.000
I don't want those weapons in the neighborhood,
I got a nephew that lives here."

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:52.700
Although Mahershala Ali provides the voice
for Uncle Aaron in Into The Spider-Verse,

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:56.740
the filmmakers found time to acknowledge Glover's
various roles in a quick Easter egg.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:01.859
When Miles goes to visit his uncle, eagle-eyed
fans can spot Aaron watching a few seconds

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:02.740
of Community.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:08.239
Specifically, "Anthropology 101," an episode
that features Glover's Troy Barnes waking

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:10.040
up in Spider-Man pajamas.

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:14.220
Throughout Into The Spider-Verse, Peter B.
Parker struggles with the mess he's made of

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:14.720
his life.

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:19.320
He's sleepwalking through his days and marking
the hours with pizza boxes and dirty laundry.

00:04:19.540 --> 00:04:24.100
His downturn is partly because of his split
from Mary Jane, for reasons that aren't explicitly

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:25.160
revealed in the film.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:30.040
However, if you pay attention, one reason
as to why they broke up becomes apparent:

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:30.920
he didn't want kids.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:35.260
It's once again a storyline slightly adjusted
from the comic book source material.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:40.060
In the '90s, Peter and MJ almost had a baby
before separating for a few years.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:43.530
In the movie, it seems like Peter's pretty
staunchly anti-spider-baby.

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:48.620
However, by the end of the film, Peter takes
a moment to wonder if he actually does want

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:49.180
kids.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:54.700
In one comics timeline, he eventually has
a daughter with MJ who he names May Parker,

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:57.200
and she naturally grows up to be Spider-Girl.

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:01.720
If Peter and MJ do get back together following
the events of Into the Spider-Verse, we wouldn't

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:06.990
be surprised to see May Parker pop up in the
sequel, or even in the upcoming Gwen Stacy

00:05:06.990 --> 00:05:07.810
Spider-Woman spinoff.

